Based on the provider.tf you provided, your environment is using LocalStack (or a similar AWS emulator) instead of a real AWS cloud. The key clues are the endpoints block pointing to http://aws:4566 and the skip_credentials_validation settings.

This changes how you should configure the OpenSearch domain resource. Here are the key adjustments and the updated main.tf:

Key Adjustments for LocalStack
Engine Version: LocalStack's OpenSearch emulation works best with Elasticsearch_7.10 (or OpenSearch versions if explicitly supported by your LocalStack version). Using a very new OpenSearch version like OpenSearch_2.11 might cause compatibility issues or timeouts in the emulator .

Access Policies: While real AWS allows you to omit access_policies, LocalStack sometimes requires an explicit policy to successfully create the domain. It's safer to include a permissive policy for the test environment.

Endpoint: Your provider maps the es endpoint to LocalStack. The aws_opensearch_domain resource will use this mapping automatically.

Updated main.tf
Replace the contents of /home/bob/terraform/main.tf with the following. This configuration is tailored to work with the LocalStack provider you have defined:

hcl
resource "aws_opensearch_domain" "datacenter_es" {
  domain_name    = "datacenter-es"
  engine_version = "Elasticsearch_7.10" # LocalStack often expects this version

  cluster_config {
    instance_type  = "t3.small.search"
    instance_count = 1
  }

  ebs_options {
    ebs_enabled = true
    volume_size = 10
    volume_type = "gp2"
  }

  # LocalStack sometimes requires an access policy to be explicitly defined
  access_policies = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action   = "es:*"
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Resource = "*"
        Principal = {
          AWS = "*"
        }
      }
    ]
  })

  # Optional tags for clarity
  tags = {
    Name        = "datacenter-es"
    Environment = "dev"
  }
}
Execution Steps
Save the file.

Run terraform init (if you haven't already).

Run terraform apply -auto-approve.

Wait for the creation process. LocalStack may take a moment to download or spin up the OpenSearch container in the background .

Once apply completes, run terraform plan. It should return "No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration."

If the plan still shows changes after the apply completes, check the LocalStack logs or the Terraform state to see if any computed attributes (like the endpoint) are being updated asynchronously.
